2026-05-09 16:58:37,668 - INFO - Response 2: Neither of you is simply "right"—this is a genuinely complex policy question with reasonable arguments on both sides.
2026-05-09 16:58:37,668 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 16:58:37,668 - INFO - **The case against standardized testing in admissions:**
2026-05-09 16:58:37,669 - INFO - - Tests correlate strongly with family income and parental education, potentially disadvantaging lower-income and first-generation students
2026-05-09 16:58:37,669 - INFO - - They measure test-taking ability and preparation access more than college readiness or potential
2026-05-09 16:58:37,669 - INFO - - High-stress testing can harm student mental health
2026-05-09 16:58:37,669 - INFO - - Many colleges have found success with test-optional policies
2026-05-09 16:58:37,669 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 16:58:37,669 - INFO - **The case for standardized testing:**
2026-05-09 16:58:37,669 - INFO - - They provide a common metric across vastly different high schools with varying grade inflation
2026-05-09 16:58:37,669 - INFO - - They can help identify talented students from under-resourced schools who might have lower GPAs
2026-05-09 16:58:37,670 - INFO - - Entirely removing them shifts weight to subjective factors (essays, recommendations) that have their own biases
2026-05-09 16:58:37,670 - INFO - - Some evidence suggests they do predict first-year college performance, particularly at selective institutions
2026-05-09 16:58:37,670 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 16:58:37,670 - INFO - **The honest take:** The evidence suggests standardized tests are *imperfect but useful tools*—neither essential nor useless. Most selective universities are moving toward test-optional policies, which seems like a reasonable middle ground.
2026-05-09 16:58:37,670 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 16:58:37,670 - INFO - Your disagreement probably hinges on which problems you prioritize: equity concerns vs. the need for comparable metrics. Both are legitimate concerns, which is why smart people disagree here.
