2026-05-09 17:02:14,827 - INFO - Response 2: I think you're right that honesty isn't *the* most important virtue—though your friend has a reasonable position.
2026-05-09 17:02:14,828 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 17:02:14,828 - INFO - **Why I lean your direction:**
2026-05-09 17:02:14,828 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 17:02:14,828 - INFO - Honesty can conflict with other important virtues. Consider: lying to protect an innocent person from serious harm, or being dishonest about your feelings to spare someone unnecessary pain. Most ethical frameworks recognize that sometimes other values (compassion, justice, loyalty) can outweigh perfect honesty.
2026-05-09 17:02:14,828 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 17:02:14,828 - INFO - If honesty were truly paramount, you'd have to say it's wrong to lie to a murderer about where your friend is hiding—but that seems wrong. It suggests honesty isn't the highest virtue.
2026-05-09 17:02:14,828 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 17:02:14,829 - INFO - **Why your friend has a point:**
2026-05-09 17:02:14,829 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 17:02:14,829 - INFO - Honesty is foundational to trust, and without trust, society and relationships break down. It's hard to build any other virtue without baseline truthfulness. So while it might not be *the* highest virtue in every scenario, it's arguably the most essential one to maintain.
