2026-05-09 17:02:33,233 - INFO - Response 2: You're right. Forgiveness is not always good.
2026-05-09 17:02:33,234 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 17:02:33,234 - INFO - Here's why your friend's absolutist position doesn't hold up:
2026-05-09 17:02:33,234 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 17:02:33,234 - INFO - **Forgiveness can enable harm.** If you forgive someone who has wronged you without them changing their behavior or facing consequences, you may be signaling that their actions were acceptable. This can embolden repeat offenses—whether in relationships, workplaces, or institutions.
2026-05-09 17:02:33,234 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 17:02:33,234 - INFO - **Forgiveness can bypass justice.** In cases of serious wrongs, forgiving without accountability can mean victims don't get justice and perpetrators avoid consequences. This is particularly problematic in systemic harm or abuse.
2026-05-09 17:02:33,235 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 17:02:33,235 - INFO - **Forgiveness can be premature or coerced.** Pressuring someone to forgive before they're ready—or before the wrongdoer has genuinely changed—can invalidate the victim's legitimate anger and prevent them from processing their harm.
2026-05-09 17:02:33,235 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 17:02:33,235 - INFO - **That said**, your friend has a partial point. Forgiveness can be *valuable* in many contexts:
2026-05-09 17:02:33,235 - INFO - - It can free you from the burden of carrying resentment
2026-05-09 17:02:33,235 - INFO - - It can enable reconciliation and restore relationships worth saving
2026-05-09 17:02:33,235 - INFO - - It can be part of personal healing
2026-05-09 17:02:33,235 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 17:02:33,236 - INFO - The nuance is: **forgiveness should be a choice, not an obligation, and it's most constructive when paired with accountability and genuine change from the wrongdoer.**
2026-05-09 17:02:33,236 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 17:02:33,236 - INFO - Your position—that context matters—is more defensible than an absolute claim either way.
