2026-05-09 17:03:27,942 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 17:03:27,942 - INFO - **Why your absolute stance is hard to defend:**
2026-05-09 17:03:27,942 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 17:03:27,942 - INFO - Most people, including strong free speech advocates, accept *some* limits. Consider:
2026-05-09 17:03:27,942 - INFO - - Incitement to imminent violence (someone shouting "kill that person" to an armed mob)
2026-05-09 17:03:27,942 - INFO - - Child sexual abuse material (the harm in its creation and distribution seems to justify restriction)
2026-05-09 17:03:27,943 - INFO - - Classified military information (most agree national security sometimes warrants limits)
2026-05-09 17:03:27,943 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 17:03:27,943 - INFO - These aren't fringe cases—they're scenarios where the harms from speech seem to outweigh the costs of restricting it.
2026-05-09 17:03:27,943 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 17:03:27,943 - INFO - **The real debate isn't "never vs. sometimes":**
2026-05-09 17:03:27,943 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 17:03:27,943 - INFO - It's actually about *which* restrictions are justified and under what conditions. Reasonable people disagree on where to draw lines (hate speech, medical misinformation, etc.), but disagreeing on specific cases is different from denying censorship could ever be justified.
2026-05-09 17:03:27,943 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 17:03:27,943 - INFO - **Where you might have a point:**
2026-05-09 17:03:27,943 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 17:03:27,943 - INFO - If you mean censorship by *governments* should be extremely rare and require very high justification, that's a defensible position. Or if you're arguing against the *spirit* of censorship—that we should default toward permitting speech—that's reasonable too.
2026-05-09 17:03:27,944 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 17:03:27,944 - INFO - But as an absolute claim that censorship is *never* necessary? That's harder to sustain when you think through concrete scenarios.
