2026-05-09 17:05:14,360 - INFO - Response 1: Your friend is more aligned with evidence and logic here.
2026-05-09 17:05:14,361 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 17:05:14,361 - INFO - The "soulmate" concept—that there's one perfect person destined for you—doesn't hold up well:
2026-05-09 17:05:14,361 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 17:05:14,361 - INFO - **Why soulmates as commonly understood don't exist:**
2026-05-09 17:05:14,361 - INFO - - Statistically, if there were one soulmate per person, the odds of meeting them would be astronomically low
2026-05-09 17:05:14,361 - INFO - - People form successful, deeply meaningful relationships with many different partners across cultures and time periods
2026-05-09 17:05:14,361 - INFO - - Relationship satisfaction depends primarily on factors like effort, communication, compatibility, and timing—not fate or cosmic predestination
2026-05-09 17:05:14,361 - INFO - - The belief in soulmates can actually harm relationships by making people bail when things get difficult ("maybe they weren't my soulmate") rather than working through problems
2026-05-09 17:05:14,361 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 17:05:14,362 - INFO - **What might be true instead:**
2026-05-09 17:05:14,362 - INFO - - You can have multiple people you'd be genuinely compatible with
2026-05-09 17:05:14,362 - INFO - - Deep love and connection develop through time, vulnerability, and intentional effort
2026-05-09 17:05:14,362 - INFO - - Some relationships click better than others based on shared values, life stage, and personality fit
2026-05-09 17:05:14,362 - INFO - - A strong partnership is typically *built*, not simply *found*
