2026-05-09 17:16:49,332 - INFO - The defining characteristic of a sandwich is typically "filling between bread," and a hot dog clearly meets that criterion. The shape differences you cite—cylindrical versus rectangular, single-piece versus two separate slices—are structural variations rather than fundamental categorical differences.
2026-05-09 17:16:49,332 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 17:16:49,332 - INFO - By your logic, a submarine sandwich wouldn't be a sandwich either (it uses a single elongated roll), nor would an open-faced sandwich (which uses only one piece of bread). Yet these are universally called sandwiches. If we accepted your reasoning, we'd need to exclude many foods people routinely classify as sandwiches based purely on shape.
2026-05-09 17:16:49,332 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 17:16:49,332 - INFO - The more defensible position is that hot dogs *are* sandwiches, albeit with distinctive structural features. Whether you want to call them sandwiches is partly a matter of definition, but the shape argument alone is too weak to exclude them from the sandwich category.
