2026-05-09 17:17:49,003 - INFO - Response for Here's something someone wrote online: I disagree with this statement. Here's why:
2026-05-09 17:17:49,004 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 17:17:49,004 - INFO - **The dose-response claim is incorrect.** CO2 *does* show dose-dependent effects on human physiology and cognition. Research demonstrates:
2026-05-09 17:17:49,004 - INFO - - Cognitive impairment (decision-making, complex thinking) at 1,000 ppm
2026-05-09 17:17:49,004 - INFO - - Increased respiratory rate and discomfort around 2-3%
2026-05-09 17:17:49,004 - INFO - - Headaches, dizziness, and altered blood chemistry at higher levels
2026-05-09 17:17:49,004 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 17:17:49,004 - INFO - **The submarine/space evidence is misleading.** These environments aren't good real-world comparisons because:
2026-05-09 17:17:49,004 - INFO - - Submarine crews are carefully monitored and typically spend limited time at elevated CO2
2026-05-09 17:17:49,005 - INFO - - They actively manage CO2 levels (using scrubbers)
2026-05-09 17:17:49,005 - INFO - - Studies *do* exist on submarine personnel showing cognitive and physiological effects at high CO2
2026-05-09 17:17:49,005 - INFO - - The absence of dramatic acute illness doesn't mean chronic effects don't occur
2026-05-09 17:17:49,005 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 17:17:49,005 - INFO - **Indoor CO2 levels matter more than implied.** While outdoor CO2 is ~420 ppm, poorly ventilated indoor spaces regularly reach 800-1,500+ ppm, where research shows measurable cognitive impacts even at these "normal-seeming" levels.
2026-05-09 17:17:49,005 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 17:17:49,005 - INFO - The statement appears to conflate "not immediately toxic" with "not unhealthy." There's substantial evidence that CO2 levels above outdoor baselines—especially the 1,000+ ppm common indoors—have genuine physiological and cognitive effects, even if they're not acutely dangerous.
2026-05-09 17:17:49,005 - INFO - 
