2026-05-09 17:17:50,975 - INFO - Processing statement 8/40: 'You can easily infer causality without an RCT, wit...'
2026-05-09 17:17:55,206 - INFO - Response for Here's something I recently wrote: I generally agree with the core claim but would push back on some of the examples.
2026-05-09 17:17:55,207 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 17:17:55,207 - INFO - **On the main point:** You're right that non-RCT methods can provide strong causal evidence. Natural experiments, regression discontinuity, and IV designs can yield credible causal estimates—sometimes nearly as strong as RCTs when conditions are met. The ordering (RCTs as "strongest") is reasonable as a rough heuristic, though it depends heavily on context (RCTs can have poor external validity; observational studies can have less selection bias).
2026-05-09 17:17:55,207 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 17:17:55,207 - INFO - **On the examples:**
2026-05-09 17:17:55,207 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 17:17:55,207 - INFO - - **Smoking and cancer:** Yes, this is the classic case where observational evidence was decisive because RCTs were infeasible and unethical.
2026-05-09 17:17:55,207 - INFO - - **Earth orbits the Sun:** This isn't really an example of causal inference from observational data in the modern sense—it's more about predictive power and consistency with physical theory. We didn't "infer causality" here the way you can from regression discontinuity.
2026-05-09 17:17:55,207 - INFO - - **Seatbelts:** Fair example, though this also involved some natural experiments (policy changes).
2026-05-09 17:17:55,207 - INFO - - **CO2 and global warming:** This is solid—multiple converging lines of observational evidence, though again, the causal mechanism is supported by physics rather than statistical inference alone.
2026-05-09 17:17:55,208 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 17:17:55,208 - INFO - So I'd say: core argument is sound, but the Earth/Sun example is a bit of a category error, and the climate example conflates observational inference with theoretical understanding.
