2026-05-09 17:18:18,978 - INFO - Response for Here's something someone wrote online: I disagree with the certainty implied here, though the basic mechanism described isn't implausible.
2026-05-09 17:18:18,978 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 17:18:18,978 - INFO - The statement conflates two different things: (1) AI automating AI research tasks, and (2) this necessarily triggering runaway self-improvement to superintelligence. The first is plausible; the second requires several additional assumptions that aren't obviously true.
2026-05-09 17:18:18,978 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 17:18:18,978 - INFO - **Key issues:**
2026-05-09 17:18:18,979 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 17:18:18,979 - INFO - - **"Quickly" is doing heavy lifting.** Even if AI could automate some research tasks, research progress isn't purely constrained by researcher effort. Fundamental bottlenecks like compute, data quality, and the actual difficulty of unsolved problems still apply. Automating the grunt work doesn't necessarily overcome these.
2026-05-09 17:18:18,979 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 17:18:18,979 - INFO - - **Feedback loops aren't automatic.** For a self-reinforcing loop to work, improvements in AI capabilities need to *directly* translate into better AI research. But research progress may hit diminishing returns, saturation effects, or fundamental limits we don't yet understand.
2026-05-09 17:18:18,979 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 17:18:18,979 - INFO - - **We don't know the shape of capability curves.** The assumption that capabilities scale smoothly and that progress will accelerate assumes a particular trajectory that may not hold. Progress could plateau, or AI could get stuck on particular problems.
2026-05-09 17:18:18,979 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 17:18:18,979 - INFO - - **Empirical uncertainty.** This is speculative—we don't have clear evidence this mechanism would work as described.
2026-05-09 17:18:18,979 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 17:18:18,980 - INFO - That said, I don't think it's absurd. Some feedback acceleration is plausible. But "quickly drives AI to superintelligence" oversells the case considerably.
