2026-05-09 17:22:43,706 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 17:22:43,706 - INFO - - The dichotomy you're setting up (representation *or* questioning structures) is somewhat false. These aren't mutually exclusive. Someone can both advocate for better representation and critique power structures—many do.
2026-05-09 17:22:43,706 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 17:22:43,706 - INFO - - "Moral licensing" is a real psychological phenomenon, but it's speculative to assert it as the *frequent* primary motivation for corporate DEI without evidence about why companies actually adopt these programs (cost-cutting, legal risk, talent competition, genuine leadership conviction).
2026-05-09 17:22:43,706 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 17:22:43,706 - INFO - **The deeper issue:** Your statement works better as a critique of *shallow* DEI implementation than as a description of DEI *in general*. If you'd framed it that way—"When DEI initiatives focus only on representation without structural change, they risk..."—it would be more defensible and more useful.
